			<div class="content" id="post-content-4475814">
									

<p>Alright, it's been about a day. Time for round two.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">And&nbsp;down&nbsp;the&nbsp;rabbit&nbsp;hole...</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">...&nbsp;we&nbsp;go!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>As you said, I was almost there. And in that spirit, I'm not throwing out everything from my last post. As of right now, the most important parts of the previous theory that carry over are the following:</p>
<ul>
<li>This article is just the ending to a game of &quot;A Gathering of One Hundred Supernatural Tales&quot;.</li>
<li>The doll in the testing log is just the merged version of Cindel and Mandy Shields.</li>
<li>By the end of the article, something got out.</li>
<li>If we wanted to survive, we should've heeded the warning and not read the article.</li>
</ul>
<p>Now, important new points that will be appropriately factored into my new theory:</p>
<ul>
<li>The blackboxes line up nicely with the kill agent protocol name from Swann's proposal, meaning that the only article directly referenced by this one is Swann's proposal</li>
<li>Everything in teletype was not supposed to be there</li>
</ul>
<p>Let us begin the new theory, shall we?</p>
<p>My dear Watson, let's examine exhibit A: the teletype. The teletype throughout the article does a few things. One of the most notable ones is that it details the weakening of the chains as we continue to read the article. But they also tell us a few other things. They tell us about Cindel and Mandy. They tell us about the witnesses. And they tell us about spirals.</p>
<p>Given the way that the teletype uniformly escalates throughout the article, I still hold my belief that by reading the article, we're freeing something. And by finishing the final story, we've unleashed whatever this thing is upon ourselves.</p>
<p>I want to also note that this thing is most like a pataphysical entity of some type, given how it infiltrates each article in a way. Most of the times it appears it comes out as something that's not really grounded in the chapter itself. It's always in a category of its own. The mentions of chains are not really tied to any one story. Cindel and Mandy are both purple, not one of the empathic profile colors. Spirals comes out of nowhere.</p>
<p>Except&#8230; well except in one place. In the exploration log, the monster the task force meets is named by the teletype as, the &quot;FALSE BEAST&quot;.</p>
<p>I don't have a lot of connective tissue for this jump, but I'm going to continue on the premise that this is the name for the anomaly that is contained by the stories. It's the thing that's breaking the chains. It's the monster that smiles in the video at the end.</p>
<p>But&#8230; but notice. I'm not calling it SCP-5000. Because it isn't. No, the FALSE BEAST is just part of the con procs for SCP-5000.</p>
<p>The <tt>spirals</tt> line feels a bit out of nowhere, no? Well, let me ask you this, with Swann's proposal in mind: What does the fractal memetic kill agent on the SCP-001 page have on it?</p>
<p>Spirals.</p>
<p>You know, there was a time when shaggy was joking about not naming this article &quot;Seven&quot;. He was joking about naming it &quot;Death of the Reader&quot;. Kind of a spin on &quot;Death of the Author&quot;. I wondered why, and I thought I had a reasonable explanation for it in my first theory, but I think I was off.</p>
<p>We aren't releasing SCP-5000.</p>
<p>We <em>are</em> SCP-5000.</p>
<p>And this is the Foundation deciding we need to be contained.</p>
<p>Before you open this document, each of these are just foundation-ized versions of classic horror story tropes. Classic ghost stories. As we read the article the embodiment of the memetic kill agent, the FALSE BEAST, is just tearing through the stories, trying to get to us. It invades and contorts what were originally standalone stories built to keep it bound, and meshes them all together as we go, bringing pieces from one into the next into the next.</p>
<p>Until we reach the end. And the kill agent greets us with a smile.</p>
</div>
